WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.907
Task statement 4.2.

2
00:00:01.907 --> 00:00:06.868
Apply few-shot prompting to improve output consistency and quality.

3
00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:15.753
Your extractor formats dates three different ways and invents tax IDs on unusual invoices; a few good examples fix both.

4
00:00:17.153 --> 00:00:24.706
Few shot prompting means putting a handful of worked examples in the prompt: an input, and the exact output you want for it.

5
00:00:24.706 --> 00:00:28.177
It is the most reliable way to get consistent formatting.

6
00:00:28.177 --> 00:00:33.294
It is like handing a new colleague three completed forms instead of a page of rules.

7
00:00:33.294 --> 00:00:37.313
They pick up both the format and how you handle the awkward cases.

8
00:00:38.713 --> 00:00:43.956
Choose a small number of examples, two to four in the guide's terms, and make them count.

9
00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:52.379
Pick the cases where a careless reader would get it wrong, and include a short reason explaining why the chosen answer beats the other options.

10
00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:57.209
Wrap each one in example tags so Claude can tell them apart from the instructions.

11
00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:01.959
For data extraction, variety matters more than volume.

12
00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:06.798
Show a table layout, a prose layout and one with details tucked in a footnote.

13
00:01:06.798 --> 00:01:11.885
Include an example where a field simply is not there and the right answer is null.

14
00:01:11.885 --> 00:01:18.833
That teaches Claude to look carefully and to admit absence, instead of leaving fields empty or making values up.

15
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:25.231
In the structured extraction scenario, invoices come from hundreds of vendors.

16
00:01:25.231 --> 00:01:30.421
The tax ID sits in a header on some, a footer on others, and is missing on a few.

17
00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:38.879
Three examples covering exactly those cases, each with a one line reason, do more than another paragraph of instructions ever could.

18
00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:47.831
When the problem is inconsistent format or shaky judgment on ambiguous inputs, the answer is usually few shot examples.

19
00:01:47.831 --> 00:01:49.544
But quality beats quantity.

20
00:01:49.544 --> 00:02:00.460
Look for options with a small number of varied examples that include reasoning, and be suspicious of answers that pile on more instructions or many near identical examples.

21
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.175
Examples teach format and judgment at the same time.

22
00:02:05.175 --> 00:02:08.490
Keep them few, varied and pointed at the hard cases.

23
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.212
Add a short reason to each.

24
00:02:10.212 --> 00:02:15.567
For extraction, cover different layouts and show what to do when a field is missing.

25
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:18.500
That is how you get consistent, honest output.

